## 1. Introduction and Historical Context

Text-based analysis in economics and finance has evolved rapidly since the early 2000s. Prior research in content analysis and computational linguistics (e.g., Holsti, 1969; Krippendorff, 1980) laid conceptual foundations. However, it was not until the mid- to late 2000s that pioneering work like Tetlock (2007) and Loughran & McDonald (2011) demonstrated how textual sentiment and word frequencies could predict market and firm performance. 

By the 2010s, economists began leveraging more specialized dictionaries and large text datasets to measure economic and policy uncertainty. Baker, Bloom, and Davis (2016) introduced one of the first widely used news-based uncertainty indices. Subsequently, researchers and institutions explored more advanced methods, including machine learning (ML) and large language models (LLMs), to classify and quantify different types of risk.

